>  I think the bigger issue here is that macOS with poll() returns HUP on a graceful close (when RecvEvent was requested). Versus the current behaviour, this means that because OUT is masked we won't send one last message to the peer before disconnecting.

Imo that could be addressed by considering a HUP to be a read event rather than an error.
```diff
diff --git a/src/util/sock.cpp b/src/util/sock.cpp
index 1e7c13e3def..c333264fdff 100644
--- a/src/util/sock.cpp
+++ b/src/util/sock.cpp
@@ -189,3 +189,3 @@ bool Sock::WaitMany(std::chrono::milliseconds timeout, EventsPerSock& events_per
         events.occurred = 0;
-        if (pfds[i].revents & POLLIN) {
+        if (pfds[i].revents & (POLLIN | POLLHUP)) {
             events.occurred |= RecvEvent;
@@ -195,3 +195,3 @@ bool Sock::WaitMany(std::chrono::milliseconds timeout, EventsPerSock& events_per
         }
-        if (pfds[i].revents & (POLLERR | POLLHUP)) {
+        if (pfds[i].revents & POLLERR {
             events.occurred |= ErrorEvent;
```

Imo that better represents with a HUP means.

In my tests on Linux, though, `POLLERR` (I think it's `POLLHUP|POLLERR` to be specific) is emitted when a remote does a full close, so catching the `HUP` doesn't do much good.

Of course, we could always fix our client-side to do a proper half-close, but I think that's out-of-scope for this discussion.
